# pixi
.pixi/
*.egg-info/
# python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
# Coverage artifacts. `test-cov` writes `.coverage`, but under xdist every worker first writes its
# own `.coverage.<host>.<pid>.<random>` and they are combined afterwards -- so the glob is not
# optional, and a run that dies mid-way leaves the per-worker files behind. Nothing here is anyone's
# deliverable: it is a line-hit database for one run on one machine, rewritten by the next.
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml
build/
dist/
# seqforge working state (resumable, content-addressed — never committed).
#
# ANCHORED, and it has to be. An unanchored `seqforge/` matches a directory of that name at ANY
# depth, which in this repo is `src/seqforge/` — git would ignore our own source tree, silently.
# The leading slash pins it to the repo root, which is where a `-C .` run puts it.
/seqforge/
.seqforge/
# docs build
site/
# editor / OS
.DS_Store
.vscode/settings.local.json

# Slurm job logs (lab convention: `mkdir -p sbatch` in the submit dir)
sbatch/

# Local scratch harnesses. `_*_test.py` / `_test_*.py` are collected by pytest but are nobody's
# deliverable -- they exist to answer one question during an investigation and then stop mattering.
# Ignored so a broad `git add` cannot sweep one into a commit, which is how `_scratch2_test.py`
# reached main twice in one PR.
/tests/_*_test.py
/tests/_test_*.py

# STAR run artifacts. STAR writes these to its `--outFileNamePrefix`, which defaults to the CWD, so
# any STAR invocation from the repo root drops them here. The whole family is listed, not just the
# `Log.out` that actually got caught: ignoring one name leaves the next stray (`Log.final.out`,
# `SJ.out.tab`, ...) sweepable by a broad `git add`, which is the failure this file keeps preventing.
/Log.out
/Log.final.out
/Log.progress.out
/SJ.out.tab
/_STARtmp/
/Aligned.out.bam
/Aligned.sortedByCoord.out.bam
/Solo.out/

# npm's leavings from the vendored Tailwind builds. `report/assets/VENDOR.md` ends its rebuild with
# `rm -rf node_modules package-lock.json`, and that is still the point -- node is a build-time tool
# and nothing from npm is a deliverable here. But the `rm` only runs if the build RUNS TO COMPLETION:
# a Ctrl-C, a failed resolve, or reading the procedure and stopping after the install all leave a
# ~50 MB tree sitting in the package source, where a broad `git add` can sweep it into the wheel.
# The whole family is listed rather than the one name that got caught, per the STAR block above.
#
# Deliberately UNANCHORED, unlike `/seqforge/`: there the leading slash is load-bearing because an
# unanchored match would swallow `src/seqforge/`. Here there is nothing in the tree these names could
# collide with, and the builds run in two different asset directories with a third always possible.
node_modules/
package-lock.json
package.json

# Browser-automation leavings. The Playwright MCP server writes screenshots, page snapshots and
# console logs to `<repo>/.playwright-mcp/`, and it refuses to write outside the repo root -- so a
# session that looks at a rendered `report.html` cannot put them in a scratch directory instead. They
# are one session's evidence, not anyone's deliverable, and a PNG in git is a binary nobody can
# review. The directory is listed rather than `*.png`: the same run drops `.yml` and `.log` beside
# the images, and ignoring one extension leaves the next stray sweepable by a broad `git add`.
/.playwright-mcp/

# Agent worktrees. Claude Code puts an isolated checkout here, INSIDE the repo, so every root-level
# tool walks into it: ruff lints another session's half-finished file and fails your gate, and
# `ruff format .` / `--fix` rewrites their working tree from under them. Ignoring it fixes both
# directions at once, because ruff respects gitignore. Same reason the scratch harnesses above are
# ignored: what is not ours must not be sweepable by a broad command.
/.claude/
